 ('C9','Terminal-value application: suppose a formal open disc lies off an ACTUAL finite core carrying the reference model, and choose its nonvanishing formal frame. Its reference norm is constant. For a genuine FS metric each section in that frame is analytic on the disc. Along any nested closed-disc path tending to a terminal analytic point, the norm of each section is nonincreasing; hence the finite weighted maximum defining each FS potential is nonincreasing. An arbitrary actual PSH potential is an infimum of a decreasing continuous FS net, so it obeys the same order, even at a pole. For every path point x_t and endpoint x, continuity of each FS approximant gives f_alpha(x_t)>=f_alpha(x), so u(x_t)>=u(x). On the other hand upper semicontinuity of u gives u(x)>=limsup_t u(x_t). The monotone limit therefore equals u(x), including the value -infinity. This proves the proposed endpoint identification AFTER the actual formal frame/path topology has been verified; it does not identify an analytically lisse function with a CLD-smooth one.'),
 ('C10','Consequently, equality of actual finite-energy solutions on all type-II points extends to every terminal point using C9, including common poles, provided the actual core/disc description covers all points. Type-III interior points additionally use convexity along the actual annulus or nested-disc path after subtracting the affine reference weight: dense finite type-II values force finiteness and ordinary continuity in the interior. Its frame and path hypotheses must also be checked. The finite graph Green-kernel tests must themselves be recognized as differences of genuine continuous psh metrics before using the accepted O217 mixed-energy integration-by-parts operator. None of C9, a finite interpolation experiment, or an abstract graph Dirichlet completion by itself grants actual arbitrary-target existence or every-solution uniqueness.'),
